  "body": "PR 4 of #32's four, covering AC 7 and closing the two defects in its Developer\nNotes (a restarted session running the branch's copy, and nothing recording\nwhich copy ran).\n\nThe agent hands over its own instruction text as a new required `definition`\nfield; `tools/review-post/` fingerprints it and matches it against `main`'s\ncopy of `.claude/agents/<agent>.md` and the branch's, then writes the answer\ninto the round record — `· definition 7972fbc65447 (branch).` — which\n`tools/review-state/` reads back into the state report as\n`Definitions last round:`.\n\nJudgement calls a reader could not infer:\n\n- **The fingerprint is whitespace-insensitive, and matching is by\n  containment.** The text arrives retyped from an agent's context, so\n  rewrapping and indentation must not change it while a changed word must.\n  Containment rather than equality is forced by the harness, which appends a\n  paragraph to every agent's instructions that no checked-in copy has: under\n  equality every honest quote read `matches neither` and the alarm meant\n  nothing. Where one matching copy contains another, the longer ran.\n- **The working tree is not a candidate copy.** Naming it `worktree` reads as\n  a copy somebody could go and look at, and it is not one. The cost is stated\n  rather than hidden: an uncommitted edit that *only appends* to an agent file\n  is indistinguishable from the harness's own appended text and records as the\n  copy it extends. Any other uncommitted edit records as `matches neither main\n  nor branch`.\n- **The definition segment precedes the held-back `(plus N similar: …)`\n  group.** That group interpolates `similar.about`, which is agent prose;\n  behind it, a crafted summary could supply a well-formed definition segment\n  that the parser preferred over the real one, forging the one fact on the\n  record an agent does not get to assert.\n- **A definition matching neither copy is recorded, not refused.** AC 7 asks\n  for recording; AC 5's list of non-zero conditions does not include this one,\n  and failing a round on a sloppy quote stops a review nobody can unstick\n  except by re-running the agent. The check still fails a round record with no\n  definition segment at all, as `hand-posted` — only the command writes one.\n- **The round-record regex moved to `state.mjs` as `parseRoundRecord`.** Two\n  places now read that record; two regexes over one string is the drift this\n  issue is about. `check.mjs` lost its private copy and imports it.\n\nKnown limit, stated rather than hidden: this verifies what the agent handed\nover against the copies that exist. An agent that `cat`s its own file instead\nof quoting its context produces a false match. That is still strictly better\nthan the prose assertion it replaces, which is what stood wrong for six rounds\nof #31 — but it is not proof.\n\n## Test plan\n\nRed first, per CONVENTIONS.md, in both commits. `node --test tests/tools/` —\n142 tests, 0 failures, up from 119 on `main`.\n\n`fc2eb2a` — nineteen new assertions, failing before the change:\n\n- `definition.test.mjs` (new) — the fingerprint survives CRLF, indentation,\n  trailing space, and frontmatter; it does not survive a changed word.\n- `payload.test.mjs` — the round record carries the fingerprint and the\n  matched copies, says `matches neither main nor branch` when nothing matched,\n  and `plan()` refuses a round with no definition.\n- `state.test.mjs` — `parseRoundRecord` reads the round, the counts, the\n  held-back count and the definition, including a held-back summary containing\n  a paren; returns `null` for a body with no definition segment; the report\n  names each agent's last definition and omits agents with no round.\n- `check.test.mjs` — a record with no definition segment fails as\n  `hand-posted`; a `matches neither` record passes.\n\n`9535ff8` — four more assertions, failing before the fixes and covering the\nthree blocking findings round 1 raised:\n\n- `definition.test.mjs` — a quote the harness wrapped matches the copy it\n  wraps; the recorded sha is the matched copy's, not the wrapped quote's; where\n  one copy contains another, the longer is the one named.\n- `state.test.mjs` — a well-formed definition segment planted in\n  `similar.about` does not become the definition the record reports, and the\n  held-back count survives it.\n\nChecked by hand against the real command (`--dry-run`, so nothing was posted).\nThis branch edits all four agent files, so `main`'s copy and the branch's\ndiffer, which is the case AC 7 exists for:\n\n- quoting `git show main:.claude/agents/review-code.md` → `definition\n  f2271329384b (main)`.\n- quoting `git show HEAD:` of the same file → `definition 7972fbc65447\n  (branch)`.\n- the same text with a harness-style paragraph appended → `definition\n  7972fbc65447 (branch)`, unchanged, which is the round-1 fix.\n- the same text with one word changed → `matches neither main nor branch`.\n- the round with `definition` omitted → `definition is required: quote your own\n  instruction text`, exit 2.\n- end to end, a round whose real segment reads `deadbeef0001 (matches neither\n  main nor branch)` and whose `similar.about` carries a forged\n  `· definition 3f9a2c1b8e04 (main, branch).` parses back as the real one.\n\nThe `(main, branch)` case — the two copies agreeing, which is every pull\nrequest that does not touch an agent file — has no live case on this branch and\nis covered by `identify`'s unit tests. Live on this pull request's own review:\nround 1 recorded `matches neither` for every agent that quoted faithfully,\nwhich is what round 1's second blocking finding was about; round 2 records\n`(branch)` for the agents that ran it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
